<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Marseillaise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">25 April <a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/april-25">On this day: 25 April</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> feared that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> would spread, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> they tried to stop it. The Kingdom of the French declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the French army fared badly at first, and Coalition armies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The text drew on &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot;, and Mameli changed its original opening, probably at Novaro's suggestion; a verse addressed to Italian women was dropped before the debut.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li><li><blockquote>It served as a battle song as well; at the Battle of Jemappes on 6 November 1792 the sans-culottes in the army charged singing &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot; and &quot;La Carmagnole&quot;, and the victory brought all of Belgium under the revolutionary armies.</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Liberation the song was so popular that it was proposed as a new national anthem, and for a short time it served as an unofficial one beside &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chant-des-partisans">Chant des Partisans</a></li><li><blockquote>The French Army still sings it, and it was sung during World War I. Valéry Giscard d'Estaing used it as his campaign song in the presidential election of 1974 and, as president, often had troops play it together with &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chant-du-depart">Le Chant du départ</a></li><li><blockquote>In Lewis Milestone's film All Quiet on the Western Front (1930) it accompanies schoolboys leaving their classroom to enlist; in Jean Renoir's La Grande Illusion (1937) and in Casablanca (1942) it is sung by Germans and drowned out by &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Glorious Revolution of 1868 overthrew Isabella II, the song had only a modest place in the First Spanish Republic (1873–1874), when &quot;<b>La Marseillaise</b>&quot; and the &quot;Himno de Garibaldi&quot; were more popular.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li></ul></section>
